<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.1.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00839</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.1.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>73fb0f08-4f77-46fa-bb4b-5efaab465401</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<para>For questions 1 - 4, use the following scenario.
</para>
<blockquote>The relationship between the amount of time a car is parked, in hours, and the cost of parking, in dollars, can be described with a function.</blockquote>
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
      <item>Identify the independent variable in this function.</item>
    </list>  
    <list>
       <item>Amount of time car is parked</item>
       <item>Cost of parking</item>
    </list>
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> The independent variable is the amount of time the car is parked.</para>
    
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
      <item>Identify the dependent variable in this function.</item>
    </list>  
    <list>
      <item>Amount of time car is parked</item>
      <item>Cost of parking</item>
    </list>
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> The dependent variable is the cost of parking.</para>
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
      <item>Describe the function with a sentence of the form “____ is a function of ____.”</item>
    </list>
    <list>
      <item>The amount of time a car is parked is a function of the cost of parking.</item>
      <item>The cost of parking is a function of the amount of time a car is parked.</item>
     </list>
    <para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> <u>The cost of parking</u> is a function of <u>the amount of time a car is parked</u>.</para>
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
      <item>Suppose it costs $3 per hour to park, with a maximum cost of $12.</item>
     </list> 
   
  
<para>Here is a graph of the situation:</para>
<para><figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/25e5503db717b9e7113c3af27bc4b60c4bb4b73a"/></media></figure></para>
<para>What does the point \((2,6)\) mean in this situation?</para>
<list>
  <item>2 hours of parking costs $6 per hour</item>
  <item>6 hours of parking costs $2 per hour</item>
  <item>2 hours of parking costs a total of $6</item>
  <item>6 hours of parking costs a total of $2</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>2 hours of parking costs $6.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>The distance a person walks, \(d\), in kilometers, is a function of time, \(t\), in minutes, since the walk began.</item>
</list>
<para>Select the <emphasis>two statements</emphasis> about the input variable of this function that are true.</para>
<list>
  <item>Distance is the input.</item>
  <item>Time of day is the input.</item>
  <item>Time since the person started walking is the input.</item>
  <item>\(t\) represents the input.</item>
  <item>\(d\) represents the input.</item>
  <item>The input is not measured in any particular unit.</item>
  <item>The input is measured in hours.</item>
  <item>For each input, there are sometimes two outputs.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Time since the person started walking is the input; \(t\) represents the input.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is the following relation a function?</item>
</list>
<para>{\((27,−3),(8,−2),(1,−1),(0,0),(1,1),(8,2),(27,3)\)}</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>No. Since the input value, 1, goes to both –1 and 1, this relation is not a function.</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is the following relation a function? Explain your answer.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="This figure shows two table that each have one column. The table on the left has the header “Name” and lists the names “Neal”, “Krystal”, “Kelvin”, “George”, “Christa”, and “Mike”. The table on the right has the header “Phone number” and lists the numbers “123-567-4389 work”, “231-378-5941 cell”, “753-469-9731 cell”, “567-534-2970 work”, “684-369-7231 cell”, “798-367-8541 cell”, and “639-847-6971 cell”. There are arrows that start at a name and points toward a number in the phone number table. The first arrow goes from Neal to 753-469-9731 cell. The second arrow goes from Krystal to a 684-369-7231 cell. The third arrow goes from Kelvin to 231-378-5941 cell. The fourth arrow goes from George to 123-567-4389 work. The fifth arrow goes from George to 639-847-6971 cell. The sixth arrow goes from Christa to 567-534-2970 work. The seventh arrow goes from Mike to 567-534-2970 work. The eighth arrow goes from Mike to 798-367-8541 cell."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ba119f3829ed1c6ac115b2828bcd349040f3e4e8"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> No, this relation is not a function. Since both George and Mike are mapped to two different phone numbers, every input does not have exactly one output value.</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is the following relation a function? Explain your answer.</item>
</list>
<para>{\((7,−3),(−5,−4),(8,−0),(0,0),(−6,4),(−2,2),(−1,3)\)}</para>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes, this relation is a function. Every input has exactly one output value.</para></section>
